    "note": "candidate-001 — first cut of @m0saic/alpine/heatmap/v1 (the 7th Alpine template). A matrix heatmap in the white card: a rows×cols grid of rounded cells, each colored on a single-hue intensity scale (a pale tint → the full color). Row labels in a left gutter (Mon–Sun), column labels along the top (W1–W8), and a 'Less → More' legend bottom-right — the GitHub contribution-calendar pattern generalized to any rows×cols data. Construction: every cell / label / legend swatch is an ABSOLUTELY-PLACED tight rect packed full-canvas via placeRects (the donut/kpi model), so a clean grid never fights nested-split quantization and the tile count is a flat rows×cols. Color scale = bucketize each value into `levels` buckets (default 5, like GitHub): bucket 0 = empty grey, 1..N = tints from pale to full. Cells are plain rounded makeColorTiles (no inline-masks). Animation: cells fade in on a top-left→bottom-right DIAGONAL cascade; reduceMotion → static board. Knobs: color (scale hue), emptyColor, levels, min/max scale bounds, cellRadius, showLegend, showValues (print value in each cell), preset. Validated valid at 1280x800.",
    "question": "First look at the Alpine heatmap. Scrub it — cells fade in on a diagonal sweep. Does the look land? Checking: (1) cell size / gap / corner radius — do the rounded cells read as a clean GitHub-style grid, or too tight / too round? (2) the 5-bucket color scale — is the pale→full ramp legible, and is the empty-cell grey right against the white card? (3) row/column labels + the Less→More legend placement. (4) diagonal cascade — premium, or would a simpler fade / column-wipe read better? Open question: default ON for showValues, or keep it off (cleaner for dense grids)? I'll iterate one nudge at a time.",
